HW-027  ESP32 GDMA: dma_stop() returns 0 while the channel is still writing into the
        caller's buffer -- and the obvious barrier does NOT fix it.

        M5Stack StickS3 (ESP32-S3-PICO-1), Zephyr 4.4.0, 2026-07-13.
        Upstream: issue #113310, PR #113311 (now two commits: dma + i2s).

        Supersedes the mutation table in HW-024. That table said "and neither is a channel
        reset". It was WRONG, and it was wrong because of how I verified it. See below.

THE QUESTION
------------
A reviewer's blocking objection to PR #113311 was, in substance:

    "The block is returned to the mem-slab immediately after dma_stop(). On ESP32-S3,
     dma_esp32_stop() disables the interrupt, writes the GDMA STOP command, and returns.
     It does not wait for the descriptor FSM to become idle. The hardware and HAL expose
     INLINK_PARK separately, and the driver already uses that bit to implement
     get_status().busy. This patch's safety depends on a timing assumption that nothing
     documents."

That is correct, and it is the sharpest thing anyone said about this patch. The fix that
had stopped the crash -- stopping the I2S RX unit before dma_stop() -- prevents the writes
empirically, but nothing says it MUST.

The reviewer's proposed remedy was a bounded wait on the descriptor FSM's PARK bit.

RESULT 1: THE REVIEWER IS RIGHT ABOUT THE PROBLEM
--------------------------------------------------
dma_esp32_stop(), verbatim:

    static int dma_esp32_stop(const struct device *dev, uint32_t channel)
    {
            ...
            gdma_ll_rx_enable_interrupt(..., false);
            gdma_ll_rx_stop(data->hal.dev, dma_channel->channel_id);
            return 0;
    }

and sixty lines below it, in the SAME driver:

    static int dma_esp32_get_status(...)
    {
            status->busy = !gdma_ll_rx_is_desc_fsm_idle(data->hal.dev, ch);

The driver knows how to observe whether the channel is busy. stop() does not look.

RESULT 2: THE PROPOSED REMEDY DOES NOT WORK
--------------------------------------------
A bounded wait on INLINK_PARK was added to dma_esp32_stop(), instrumented to count its
own spins.

    dma_stop() calls                              890
    ...that returned with the FSM still running     0
    worst-case spins waiting for PARK               0

The FSM had ALWAYS already parked. Not once did the wait actually wait.

And with the I2S RX unit left running, the DMA wrote into the freed block anyway:

    c1.canary  stamped 8 free rx blocks, waited 50 ms, 1 came back written to
    c2.canary  stamped 8 free rx blocks, waited 50 ms, 1 came back written to
    ...
    ** CPU 0 EXCCAUSE 28 (load prohibited)          <- cycle 5

So INLINK_PARK observes the DESCRIPTOR FSM -- "nothing left to fetch" -- and says nothing
about bytes still in flight. It is the wrong observable. A wait on it is a fix that fixes
nothing while looking like a proof, and shipping it would have told a reviewer the patch
depends on a barrier semantics that does not exist.

Corroboration, from the hardware itself: the ESP32-P4's AXI DMA has an ABORT bit ("stop
the undergoing transfer immediately") and an "is reset available" status, both DISTINCT
from stop and from park. The S3's AHB GDMA has neither. Espressif had to add a separate
abort and a separate reset-ready signal on a later part, alongside an existing park bit.

RESULT 3: WHAT DOES WORK -- AND THE VERIFICATION BUG THAT HID IT
-----------------------------------------------------------------
gdma_ll_rx_reset_channel() is documented as "Reset DMA RX channel FSM and FIFO pointer".
The FIFO is exactly the state the park bit cannot see.

I ran that experiment, reported that it did NOT work, and wrote it into HW-024. That
report was false. I "verified" that the reset had been applied with

    grep -c 'gdma_ll_rx_reset_channel' dma_esp32_gdma.c   ->  2

and concluded the patch was in. But the PRISTINE driver already contains two: one in
dma_esp32_config_rx() and one in dma_esp32_reload(). The count proved nothing, the patch
had in fact failed to apply, and the "channel reset does not help either" run was a build
with no channel reset in it.

Re-run, verifying the resets INSIDE dma_esp32_stop() (pristine: 0):

    in dma_esp32_stop(): 0     <- pristine
    in dma_esp32_stop(): 2     <- patched, aborts the build if not

    c1.canary  stamped 8 free rx blocks, waited 50 ms, 0 came back written to
    c2.canary  stamped 8 free rx blocks, waited 50 ms, 0 came back written to
    *** I2S SLAB STRESS PASSED ***

WITH THE I2S RX UNIT LEFT RUNNING. The channel reset alone is sufficient.

THE MUTATION MATRIX, RE-MEASURED
---------------------------------
Every build had its mutation VERIFIED PRESENT IN THE SOURCE before it was flashed, because
a name grep is not a verification and this file exists partly to say so.

    build                                   canary    slab          STOP/DRAIN tail  verdict
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    stock upstream                          1/8 dirty LEAKS 8,7,6.. drains           FAILED
    dma_stop() + wait for PARK              1/8 dirty --            --               FAILED (crash)
    I2S peripheral stop only                0/8       flat          drains           PASSED
    DMA channel reset only                  0/8       flat          drains           PASSED
    I2S fix + i2s_hal_tx_stop()             0/8       flat          0 of 27          FAILED
    I2S fix, RX hand-off removed            0/8       rx=9 of 8     drains           FAILED
    BOTH (what ships)                       0/8       flat 8/8      35 of 35         PASSED

Rows 3 and 4 are the point: EITHER barrier alone is sufficient. They are independent. But
only one of them has a register behind it. Stopping the producer prevents the writes; the
channel reset makes them impossible.

WHAT SHIPS, AND WHY
-------------------
PR #113311 is now two commits.

  drivers: dma: esp32: reset the channel in dma_stop()
      The contract fix. Zephyr's DMA API says dma_stop() "stops the DMA transfer and
      disables the channel", so a caller that gets 0 back may reuse the buffer -- and this
      makes that true. It costs a restarted transfer nothing: dma_esp32_start() re-arms the
      descriptor address and the interrupt, the descriptor list is in RAM, and the driver
      ALREADY resets the channel to arm a transfer in config_*() and reload(). This applies
      the same primitive where it disarms one. Every ESP32 DMA client that frees a buffer
      after dma_stop() has this exposure; I2S is just the one that was caught.

  drivers: i2s: esp32: return the DMA's in-flight mem-slab block
      The leak, the ownership hand-off, the err check, and the peripheral-stop ordering.
      The RX unit is stopped before the DMA because that is the order ESP-IDF's own I2S
      driver uses and because leaving it clocking into a FIFO nobody reads is wrong on its
      own terms -- NOT because the memory safety rests on it. It rests on the reset.

The park wait ships nowhere. scripts/repro_dma_park_is_not_a_barrier.sh reproduces the
negative result and says so in its name.

AND THE CALIBRATION THAT KEPT ME OUT OF A HOLE
-----------------------------------------------
I was going to argue "ESP-IDF's own I2S teardown does peripheral-stop then gdma_stop,
therefore that sequence is sufficient". It is not a safe argument. esp-idf#18640 is OPEN:
after i2s_channel_disable() -- exactly that sequence -- the GDMA channel is still dirty
enough to corrupt a subsequent DIFFERENT consumer of the same physical channel (hardware
AES/SHA). Espressif's own maintainer, in that thread: "gdma_reset won't reset registers.
It only reset the FIFO and FSM."

So the PR cites ESP-IDF's ORDERING, which is narrow and defensible, and rests the memory
safety on the reset, which is measured. It does not claim to fix #18640, which is a
different failure mode.

