=== I2S stress: 100 cycles; TX starved every 5, STOP every 3, DRAIN every 7

slab  : free blocks per direction, read BEFORE each cycle. It must stay

        at 8. Falling means the driver is losing the DMA's in-flight

        block on every DROP; rising above 8 means a block was freed

        twice and the free list has a cycle in it.

starve: TX is left to run dry, which drives the driver down its tx_disable

        path -- one of the places the leak fix could double-free.

STOP  : and DRAIN. BOTH reach I2S_STATE_STOPPING (the driver falls through

        from one case to the other); DROP goes straight to READY under

        irq_lock and never gets there at all. STOPPING is where the RX

        callback hands its block to the queue while still pointing at it,

        so a DROP-only stress cannot see that path.

DRAIN : not a duplicate of STOP. STOP sets tx_stop_without_draining, so TX

        stops after its in-flight block; DRAIN does not, so TX keeps pulling

        blocks off its queue and re-arming DMA until it is empty -- and every

        one of those restarts overlaps an RX ownership hand-off.

tail  : a STOP or DRAIN that drains NOTHING is a FAILURE, not a note. The

        STOPPING branch always queues its final block before it stops, so an

        empty tail read means it never ran. The slab census cannot see that:

        this test printed 'drained 0' for 27 cycles and then printed PASSED.

